proof.text:
Construct the module associator $a_{M,N,P}$ by forgetting the right action in the composite
$$
J((M\boxtimes N)\boxtimes P)\xrightarrow{\theta_{M\boxtimes N,P}}
J(M\boxtimes N)\odot JP\xrightarrow{\theta_{M,N}\odot 1}
(JM\odot JN)\odot JP\xrightarrow{a^{\mathcal B}}
JM\odot(JN\odot JP)\xrightarrow{1\odot\theta_{N,P}^{-1}}
JM\odot J(N\boxtimes P)\xrightarrow{\theta_{M,N\boxtimes P}^{-1}}
J(M\boxtimes(N\boxtimes P)).
$$
Here $a^{\mathcal B}$ is the natural bimodule associator of [lem-sfb-bimod-monoidal], and all comparisons are the identity isomorphisms of [def-sfb-symmetric-bimod-module-iso]. Take the left unitor to underlie $J(I\boxtimes M)\xrightarrow{\theta_{I,M}}JI\odot JM=\mathcal R\odot JM\xrightarrow{l^{\mathcal B}}JM$, and the right unitor to underlie $J(M\boxtimes I)\xrightarrow{\theta_{M,I}}JM\odot JI\xrightarrow{r^{\mathcal B}}JM$. The inverse composites are bimodule maps as well, so these are module isomorphisms. They are the required constraints induced from bimodules.
